1 NOTE My 26th great grand father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi and eminent scholar.
2 CONT Lord of Vives.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Rabbi Meir ben Samuel and Lord Thibault of Provins met on the road to Jerusalem. Both recognized in the other "un homme de condition" (a nobleman) -  Meir ben Samuel was the "sire de Vives" (lord of Vives, near Meaux, France) - a
2 CONC nd soon they got acquainted. Since both where travelling for "holy reasons" they rapidly grew closer. Indeed Lord Thibault was interested in helping securing the Kingdom of Jerusalem, while Rabbi Meir was on the road to replace hi
2 CONC s father-in-law, wanted as councillor by the King of Jerusalem, since he was too old for such a long trip.
2 CONT When travelling in Muslim areas, Meir and Thibault of Provins shared the same room "par crainte de traistrerie" (out of fear of being killed by Muslims at night).
2 CONT They even discovered that they were remote cousins, since Rabbi Meir was married to Yocheved, Rashi’s daughter. From that moment on they were so attached that after their return from the Holy Land, Rabbi Meir moved from Ramerupt t
2 CONC o Provins where he founded of one the biggest Jewish schools in France. This is why he became known as Rabbi Meir of Provins. His sons were the Ribam, the Rashbam and Rabbenou Tam."
2 CONT
2 CONT Founded a school in Ramerupt, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Shlomo Yitzhaki TZARFATI.
1 NOTE Bio notes: Horowitz Chart G17 Bobov Tree, Lamed-Alef (Yud-Gimmel)
2 CONT Unbroken Chain p. 688. Finding Our Fathers p. 12
