1 NOTE {geni:about_me} --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac ben Samuel the Elder, also known as the Ri ha-Zaken, was a French tosafist and Biblical commentator. He flourished at Ramerupt and Dampierre, France in the twelfth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT On his father's side Isaac was a grandson of R. Simhah ben Samuel of Vitry, author of the Maḥzor Vitry; on his mother's side he was a nephew of Rabbeinu Tam, of Rashbam, and of Isaac ben Meir (RiBaM), a great-grandson of Rashi, an
2 CONC d a relative of R. Eleazar of Worms.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was surnamed "ha-Zaḳen" (the elder) to distinguish him from another tosafist of the same name, Isaac ben Abraham, surnamed "ha-Baḥur" (the younger).
2 CONT
2 CONT He is often quoted as R. Isaac of Dampierre (Maimuniyyot, Ma'akalot Asurot, No. 5; Shibbole ha-Leḳeṭ ii., No. 40), but it seems that he lived first at Ramerupt, where his maternal grandfather resided (Sefer ha-Nayyar, p. 162; Maim
2 CONC uniyyot, l.c.). It was also at Ramerupt that he studied under his uncle R. Tam (Luria, Responsa, No. 29); after the latter had gone to Troyes, Isaac b. Samuel directed his school.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac settled at Dampierre later, and founded there a flourishing and well-attended school (Or Zarua, i. 126). It is said that he had sixty pupils, each of whom, besides being generally well grounded in Talmud, knew an entire trea
2 CONC tise by heart, so that the whole Talmud was stored in the memories of his pupils (Menahem, Ẓedah la-Derek, Introduction).
2 CONT
2 CONT As he lived under Philip Augustus, at whose hands the Jews suffered much, Isaac prohibited the buying of confiscated Jewish property, and ordered that any so bought be restored to its original owner. A particular interest attaches
2 CONC  to one of his responsa, in which he relies on the oral testimony of his aunt, the wife of R. Isaac b. Meïr, and on that of the wife of R. Eleazar of Worms, a great-granddaughter of Rashi (Sefer ha-Nayyar, p. 167a).
2 CONT
2 CONT He died, according to Heinrich Graetz (Gesch. vi. 210), about 1200; according to Henri Gross (Gallia Judaica, p. 161, and "R. E. J." vii. 76), between 1185 and 1195; and as he is known to have reached an advanced age, Gross suppos
2 CONC es that he was not born later than 1115. On the other hand, Michael (Or ha-Ḥayyim, p. 512) says that as Isaac b. Samuel was spoken of as "the sainted master" (Sefer ha-Terumah, §§ 131, 161; Tos., Zeb. 12b, 59b), a term generally g
2 CONC iven to martyrs, he may have been killed at the same time as his son Elhanan (1184).
2 CONT
2 CONT Tosafot
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac's tosafot completed the commentary of Rashi on the Talmud (Romm included in his edition of the Talmud Isaac ben Samuel's tosafot on Ḳiddushin). He also compiled and edited with great erudition all the preceding explanations 
2 CONC to Rashi's commentary. His first collection was entitled Tosefot Yeshanim, which, however, was afterward revised and developed. He is quoted on almost every page of the Tosafot, and in various works, especially in the Sefer ha-Ter
2 CONC umah of his pupil Baruch ben Isaac of Worms, and in the Or Zaṙua of Isaac ben Moses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac is mentioned as a Biblical commentator by Judah ben Eliezer (Minḥat Yehudah, p. 8b), who quotes also a work of Isaac's entitled Yalḳuṭe Midrash (ib. p. 22a); by Isaac ha-Levi; by Hezekiah ben Manoah in his Ḥazzeḳuni; and in 
2 CONC two other commentaries (see Kerem Ḥemed, vii. 68). Isaac b. Samuel is supposed to be the author also of several liturgical poems, of a piyyuṭ to the Hafṭarah (Landshuth, Ammude ha-'Abodah, p. 108), and of a piyyuṭ for Purim (Maḥzo
2 CONC r Vitry, No. 255; comp. Luzzatto in Berliner's Magazin, v. 27, Hebr. part). The authorship of these piyyuṭim may, however, belong to the liturgical writer Isaac ben Samuel of Narbonne.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Jewish Encyclopedia bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Azulai, Shem ha-Gedolim, i.;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Michael, Or haḤayyim, pp. 511-513;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Isaac Hirsch Weiss, Dor, iv. 286, 342, 349;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Heinrich Grätz, Gesch. 3d ed., vi. 210, 211, 214;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henri Gross, Gallia Judaica, pp. 161-168, 638;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * idem, in R. E. J. vii. 76;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Adolf Neubauer, ib. xvii. 67;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Leopold Zunz, Z. G. p. 33, passim.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT According to Tzvi Harkavy, Yitzhak Hazaken was the son of Shmuel, the brother-in-law of Rabenu Tam and the Rashbam.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac ben Samuel the Elder, also known as the Ri ha-Zaken, was a French tosafist and Biblical commentator. He flourished at Ramerupt and Dampierre, France in the twelfth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT On his father's side Isaac was a grandson of R. Simhah ben Samuel of Vitry, author of the Maḥzor Vitry; on his mother's side he was a nephew of Rabbeinu Tam, of Rashbam, and of Isaac ben Meir (RiBaM), a great-grandson of Rashi, an
2 CONC d a relative of R. Eleazar of Worms.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was surnamed "ha-Zaḳen" (the elder) to distinguish him from another tosafist of the same name, Isaac ben Abraham, surnamed "ha-Baḥur" (the younger).
2 CONT
2 CONT He is often quoted as R. Isaac of Dampierre (Maimuniyyot, Ma'akalot Asurot, No. 5; Shibbole ha-Leḳeṭ ii., No. 40), but it seems that he lived first at Ramerupt, where his maternal grandfather resided (Sefer ha-Nayyar, p. 162; Maim
2 CONC uniyyot, l.c.). It was also at Ramerupt that he studied under his uncle R. Tam (Luria, Responsa, No. 29); after the latter had gone to Troyes, Isaac b. Samuel directed his school.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac settled at Dampierre later, and founded there a flourishing and well-attended school (Or Zarua, i. 126). It is said that he had sixty pupils, each of whom, besides being generally well grounded in Talmud, knew an entire trea
2 CONC tise by heart, so that the whole Talmud was stored in the memories of his pupils (Menahem, Ẓedah la-Derek, Introduction).
2 CONT
2 CONT As he lived under Philip Augustus, at whose hands the Jews suffered much, Isaac prohibited the buying of confiscated Jewish property, and ordered that any so bought be restored to its original owner. A particular interest attaches
2 CONC  to one of his responsa, in which he relies on the oral testimony of his aunt, the wife of R. Isaac b. Meïr, and on that of the wife of R. Eleazar of Worms, a great-granddaughter of Rashi (Sefer ha-Nayyar, p. 167a).
2 CONT
2 CONT He died, according to Heinrich Graetz (Gesch. vi. 210), about 1200; according to Henri Gross (Gallia Judaica, p. 161, and "R. E. J." vii. 76), between 1185 and 1195; and as he is known to have reached an advanced age, Gross suppos
2 CONC es that he was not born later than 1115. On the other hand, Michael (Or ha-Ḥayyim, p. 512) says that as Isaac b. Samuel was spoken of as "the sainted master" (Sefer ha-Terumah, §§ 131, 161; Tos., Zeb. 12b, 59b), a term generally g
2 CONC iven to martyrs, he may have been killed at the same time as his son Elhanan (1184).
2 CONT
2 CONT Tosafot
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac's tosafot completed the commentary of Rashi on the Talmud (Romm included in his edition of the Talmud Isaac ben Samuel's tosafot on Ḳiddushin). He also compiled and edited with great erudition all the preceding explanations 
2 CONC to Rashi's commentary. His first collection was entitled Tosefot Yeshanim, which, however, was afterward revised and developed. He is quoted on almost every page of the Tosafot, and in various works, especially in the Sefer ha-Ter
2 CONC umah of his pupil Baruch ben Isaac of Worms, and in the Or Zaṙua of Isaac ben Moses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac is mentioned as a Biblical commentator by Judah ben Eliezer (Minḥat Yehudah, p. 8b), who quotes also a work of Isaac's entitled Yalḳuṭe Midrash (ib. p. 22a); by Isaac ha-Levi; by Hezekiah ben Manoah in his Ḥazzeḳuni; and in 
2 CONC two other commentaries (see Kerem Ḥemed, vii. 68). Isaac b. Samuel is supposed to be the author also of several liturgical poems, of a piyyuṭ to the Hafṭarah (Landshuth, Ammude ha-'Abodah, p. 108), and of a piyyuṭ for Purim (Maḥzo
2 CONC r Vitry, No. 255; comp. Luzzatto in Berliner's Magazin, v. 27, Hebr. part). The authorship of these piyyuṭim may, however, belong to the liturgical writer Isaac ben Samuel of Narbonne.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Jewish Encyclopedia bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT * Azulai, Shem ha-Gedolim, i.;
2 CONT
2 CONT * Michael, Or haḤayyim, pp. 511-513;
2 CONT
2 CONT * Isaac Hirsch Weiss, Dor, iv. 286, 342, 349;
2 CONT
2 CONT * Heinrich Grätz, Gesch. 3d ed., vi. 210, 211, 214;
2 CONT
2 CONT * Henri Gross, Gallia Judaica, pp. 161-168, 638;
2 CONT
2 CONT * idem, in R. E. J. vii. 76;
2 CONT
2 CONT * Adolf Neubauer, ib. xvii. 67;
2 CONT
2 CONT * Leopold Zunz, Z. G. p. 33, passim.
2 CONT
