1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mordechai_ben_Hillel '''Mordechai ben Hillel HaKohen'''] (c. 1250–1298), also known as The Mordechai, was a 13th-century German rabbi and posek. His chief legal commentary on the Talm
2 CONC ud, referred to as The Mordechai, is one of the sources of the Shulchan Aruch. He was killed in the Rintfleisch massacres in 1298. His principal teacher was Meïr b. Baruch of Rothenburg, of whose older pupils Mordecai was one, and
2 CONC  in whose presence Mordecai pronounced independent decisions. Mordecai quotes the tosafot, responsa, and compendiums of his teacher, together with many of his oral and written communications. Aside from Meïr must be mentioned as M
2 CONC ordecai's teachers Perez b. Elijah of Corbeille, Ephraim b. Nathan, Jacob ha-Levi of Speyer (probably identical with Jacob b. Moses ha-Levi), Abraham b. Baruch (Meïr of Rothenburg's brother), and Dan, probably identical with Dan A
2 CONC shkenazi.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/10991-mordecai-b-hillel-b-hillel '''The Mordecai'''] is generally known as the author of the great legal code "Sefer ha-Mordekai," commonly called briefly "Mordekai," or designated as th
2 CONC e "Great" or "Long Mordekai" ("Mordekai ha-Gadol," "Mordekai he-'Aruk") as distinguished from Samuel Schlettstadt's "Small Mordekai" ("Mordekai ha-Ḳaṭon"). The "Mordekai" is met with in the form of glosses to Alfasi's "Halakot" in
2 CONC  various manuscripts, and also as an appendix to the "Halakot" in many editions. This connection with Alfasi is, however, merely an external one, single sentences, sometimes even single words, of the "Halakot" serving as catchword
2 CONC s introducing the relevant material found in Yerushalmi, the French and German tosafot, the codices and compendiums. Mordecai's range of reading in halakic literature was phenomenal. There were few noteworthy works dealing with ha
2 CONC lakic subjects and antedating the middle of the thirteenth century which he did not know and draw upon. As regards the German and French authors, he knew not only all the works that are still extant, but many for which he is now t
2 CONC he only source. He quotes about 350 authorities, whose works and written or verbal communications form the substance of his book.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
