1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isaac_Abarbanel
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jewish philosopher and statesman Isaac ben Judah Abravanel (1437-1508), or Abarbanel, is noted for his biblical commentaries and for his attempt to prevent the expulsion of the Jews from Spain in 1492.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Abravanel, a descendant of an old and distinguished Spanish family, was born in Lisbon, Portugal. In addition to intensive religious training, he received a broad liberal education and acquired a thorough grounding in Greek,
2 CONC  Latin, and Christian literature. Like his father, Isaac was highly successful in both his commercial and diplomatic careers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He served as treasurer under the Portuguese kings Alfonso V and John II. Falsely charged with plotting against the monarchy, Abravanel fled in 1483 to Castile, Spain. There he devoted himself to his commentary on several biblical 
2 CONC books of the prophets.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1490 Abravanel was appointed treasurer to the Spanish monarchs Ferdinand and Isabella. But in 1492 Torquemada, the head of the Spanish Inquisition, persuaded the royal couple to expel the Jews from Spain. Despite Abravanel's im
2 CONC portant services to the Crown, his attempts to have the decree of expulsion revoked were unsuccessful. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He went into exile with his fellow Jews and moved to Naples, where he was soon given a financial post in the government. In 1495 a French invasion forced him to leave Naples. After some years of intermittent wandering, he settled 
2 CONC in Venice in 1503. He died there in 1508 and was buried in Padua.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===His Writings
2 CONT
2 CONT Abravanel's most important works are the commentaries which he wrote on almost all the books of the Old Testament. He employed what might be termed a critical or scientific approach in his biblical studies. He examined the histori
2 CONC cal episodes in the Bible in the light of economic, political, and social factors and often drew analogies to his own times. In dating biblical books, he often deviated from tradition, and he did not hesitate to consult the works 
2 CONC of Christian scholars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abravanel also wrote a number of philosophical and theological works. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His '''Rosh Amana (Pillars of Faith)''' and
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sefer Mifalot Elohim (Book of God's Works)''' show the influence of the 12th-century Jewish philosopher Maimonides. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In general Abravanel developed a negative view of culture and civilization. He was influenced by the Stoics in his condemnation of luxurious living and by the Cynics in his criticism of the political state. His pessimism was balan
2 CONC ced, however, by a firm belief in the miraculous coming of the Messiah, which he expounded in Maayene Hayeshuah (Founts of Salvation), Yeshuath Meshiho (Salvation of His Messiah), and Mashmia Yeshua (Proclaimer of Salvation). Thes
2 CONC e works contributed to the subsequent rise of false messiahs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abrabanel was a financial advisor to King Alfonso V of Portugal, although he was forced to leave the country because of charges that he participated in a conspiracy against Alfonso's successor, Joao II. Thereupon, he settled in Ca
2 CONC stille and became a diplomat and advisor in the court of Ferdinand and Isabella. Here he attempted to hinder the decree expelling the Jews, although without success. As the most influential leader of the exiles, he first settled i
2 CONC n Naples and later in Venice. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His writings include detailed, comprehensive commentaries on most books in the Hebrew Bible as well as a Messianic Trilogy in which he proposed that the distress of his time symbolized the imminence of the Messianic redemption who
2 CONC se realization he expected even in his lifetime. Influenced by the humanism of the Renaissance, he maintained contact with contemporary Christian scholars and often applied their views in his commentaries, some of which were trans
2 CONC lated into Latin and had a degree of influence in Italy. His fundamental attitude was rationalist, but he insisted that religious tradition is the ultimate source of truth. His son Judah was also a philosopher.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Dan
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT *A.J. Heschel, Don Jizchak Abravanel, 1937
2 CONT
2 CONT *J.S. Minkin, Abravanel and the Expulsion of the Jews from Spain, 1938
2 CONT
2 CONT *B. Netanyahu, Don Isaac Abravanel, 1968.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Further Reading
2 CONT
2 CONT The major scholarly work on Abravanel is B. Netanyahu, Don Isaac Abravanel: Statesman and Philosopher (1953), which contains an extensive bibliography. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://www.jewish-history.com/occident/volume8/apr1850/abarbanel.html T'''he Family of Abarbanel''']
2 CONT   Much has been said about the alleged loss of the knowledge of the family of David. But it would seem that there are many now living who actually claim a descent from the King of Israel, and whose claim is actually acknowledged. 
2 CONC We had for some time past intended to copy the following piece from the conversion organ of London, but our space wouldnot permit us. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At present, however, we have unexpectedly more room than we thought we should have, and therefore lay the extract before our readers as a curious memorandum, which they may rely on with the more certainty, as it met our eye for th
2 CONC e first time in a magazine which bears in every page the amplest testimony of its hatred of our religion, and is supported by funds contributed to effect the apostacy of our nation. “Let our enemies be judges,” says the Bible, and
2 CONC  we may freely repeat it; for, even then we have nothing to fear.—Ed. Oc.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===(From the Jewish Intelligence of Nov. 1849.)
2 CONT
2 CONT >>''The family of Abarbanel, the celebrated Jewish commentator, is remarkable for having carefully preserved its pedigree, according to which they trace their descent from king David. Hence, in all the documents and books of Abarb
2 CONC anel and his family, they have ever added to their signature: מגזע דוד מלך ישראל (from the stem of David, king of Israel).
2 CONT
2 CONT >>''Don Isaac Abarbanello, whose family had long lived in Spain, and who was permitted by authority to have a lion in his crest, was expelled from that country with the rest of the Jews, under Ferdinand the Catholic, in 1492. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>''The Abarbanel family settled finally in the East, where strangers from Christian countries were then called “Franks,” and when many years afterwards the Abarbanel family left the East, and settled in Vienna, they were still de
2 CONC signated '''“Franks,”''' with the peculiar Austrian provincial diminutive “el,” and the family has ever since retained the name '''“Frankel.”'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>''The accomplishments and intelligence for which the Abarbanel (Frankel) family was ever renowned, soon distinguished them also in Austria, both in a pecuniary and intellectual point of view. Nevertheless, they were not exempted
2 CONC  from the hard fate which the Jews met with under Leopold, in being, on February 14th, 1670, expelled from the Austrian dominions, under pain of death. (See “Jewish Intelligence” for December, 1845.).
2 CONT
2 CONT The family Frankel united with some others of their expelled brethren, in presenting a petition to the resident minister of the elector of Brandenburg, Andreas Neumann, begging that he would intercede with his royal master, in ord
2 CONC er to obtain permission for some of them to settle in his dominions.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this petition they complained that though God had created the earth for all men, yet the countries were everywhere closed against them, so that they knew not whither to turn their steps.
2 CONT The poverty and very great depopulation occasioned in the marquisate of Brandenburg, by the Thirty Years’ War, induced the elector to grant their request. The elector instructed his plenipotentiary, under the date of April 19th, 1
2 CONC 670, that he had no objection to allow forty or fifty families to settle in his dominions. On the 21st of May an edict was issued, which gave them permission to settle in the country, and carry on their mode of worship in private 
2 CONC houses, but not in public synagogues.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a characteristic of the times, it may be noticed that in the special charter, which was granted to various families, there are these restrictive clauses: “that they are to abstain from usury, and not to take more interest than 
2 CONC three pence a week for one dollar (360 pence); to abstain from purchasing stolen goods, and blaspheming Christ;” with a few other similar clauses, all calculated to demoralize the Jews.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of the members of the Frankel family settled in Berlin, others in Frankfurt-on-the-Oder, and others in Dessau,—in which latter place they founded the congregation which flourished so abundantly in after times.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Printing Press
2 CONT
2 CONT The family became the founders of very important printing offices for Jewish literature, in Berlin, Frankfurt, Jessnitz, and Dessau, whence proceeded the Talmud in three complete editions (in Berlin), and also the Pentateuch in va
2 CONC rious editions, as well as the works of Maimonides (in Jessnitz), his מורה נבוכים, as well as a complete edition of the Jerusalem Talmud, and its excellent commentary, קרבן עדת, by the grandfather of Mr. F., now living at Dessau, 
2 CONC 75 years of age. All these works are celebrated for their clearness and correctness, and often quoted by Christian writers.
2 CONT
2 CONT The commentator on the Jerusalem Talmud, just referred to, was afterwards Chief Rabbi in Berlin, and was followed thither by the juvenile Moses, son of Mendel, for the privilege of studying under so great a rabbi the Talmud and ot
2 CONC her Jewish literature. This pupil was Moses Mendelssohn, afterwards the celebrated modern reformer of the Jews.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isaac_Abrabanel    Isaac Abarabanel was born in Lisbon, Portugal into one of the oldest and most distinguished Jewish Iberian families, the Abravanel family, who had escaped persecution in Spain durin
2 CONC g 1391. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A student of the Rabbi of Lisbon, Joseph Chaim a/k/a Yosef ben Shlomo Ibn Yahya, poet, religious scholar, rebuilder of Ibn Yahya Synagogue of Calatayud (a descendant of Hiyya al-Daudi who was great-grandson of Hezekiah Gaon), he b
2 CONC ecame well versed in rabbinic literature and in the learning of his time, devoting his early years to the study of Jewish philosophy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Abravanel is quoted as saying that he included Joseph ibn Shem-Tov as his mentor. At twenty years old, he wrote on the original form of the natural elements, on religious questions and prophecy. Together with his intellectual abil
2 CONC ities, he showed a complete mastery of financial matters. This attracted the attention of King Afonso V of Portugal who employed him as treasurer.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Other works are:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * "The Crown of the Ancients"
2 CONT
2 CONT     * "The Pinnacle of Faith"
2 CONT
2 CONT     * "The Wellsprings of Redemption", in the form of a commentary on Daniel,
2 CONT
2 CONT     * "The Salvation of His Anointed"
2 CONT
2 CONT     * "The Herald of Salvation", in which are collected and explained all the Messianic texts.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * "Inheritance of the Fathers"
2 CONT
2 CONT     * "The Forms of the Elements"
2 CONT
2 CONT     * "New Heavens"
2 CONT
2 CONT     * "Deeds of God"
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://boards.ancestry.com/surnames.abravanel/1.2/mb.ashx  ''' DNA testing by descendant'''.]
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://wiki.geni.com/index.php/Jewish_Dynasties  ''Jewish Dynasties]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The Abarbanel family (also Abravanel and Abrabanel) is one of the oldest and most distinguished Jewish Iberian families; they trace their origin from the biblical King David. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Isaac is best well known for being the treasurer to King Ferdinand and Queen Isabella. Unable to prevent the expulsion of the Jews from Spain, he left with the others of his faith in 1492.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Isaac was a major commentator and his numerous texts are still treasured today.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Abarbanel was born in Lisbon, Portugal, into one of the oldest and most distinguished Jewish Iberian families, the Abravanel or Abarbanel family, who had escaped massacre in Castile in 1391. A student of the rabbi of Lisbon, Josep
2 CONC h Chaim, he became well versed in rabbinic literature and in the learning of his time, devoting his early years to the study of Jewish philosophy. Abarbanel is quoted as saying that he included Joseph ibn Shem-Tov as his mentor. A
2 CONC t twenty years old, he wrote on the original form of the natural elements, on religious questions and prophecy. Together with his intellectual abilities, he showed a complete mastery of financial matters. This attracted the attent
2 CONC ion of King Afonso V of Portugal who employed him as treasurer.
2 CONT
2 CONT He used his high position and the great wealth he had inherited from his father, to aid his co-religionists. When his patron, Afonso captured the city of Arzila, in Morocco, the Jewish captives faced being sold as slaves. Abarbane
2 CONC l contributed largely to the funds needed to free them, and personally arranged for collections throughout Portugal. He also wrote to his learned and wealthy friend, Vitale (Yehiel) Nissim da Pisa, on behalf of the captives.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Afonso he was obliged to relinquish his office, having been accused by King John II of connivance with the Duke of Braganza, who had been executed on the charge of conspiracy. Abarbanel, warned in time, saved hi
2 CONC mself by a hasty flight to Castile in 1483. His large fortune was confiscated by royal decree.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Toledo, his new home, he occupied himself at first with Biblical studies, and in the course of six months produced an extensive commentary on the books of Joshua, Judges, and Samuel. But shortly afterward he entered the service
2 CONC  of the house of Castile. Together with his friend, the influential Don Abraham Senior, of Segovia, he undertook to farm the revenues and to supply provisions for the royal army, contracts that he carried out to the entire satisfa
2 CONC ction of Queen Isabella.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Moorish war, Abarbanel advanced considerable sums of money to the king. When the Jews were ordered banished from Spain with the Alhambra decree, he did all in his power to induce the king to revoke the edict. He unsucce
2 CONC ssfully offered the king 30,000 ducats ($68,400 nominal value). He left Spain with his fellow Jews and went to Naples where, soon after, he entered the service of the king. For a short time he lived in peace undisturbed; but when 
2 CONC the city was taken by the French, bereft of all his possessions, he followed the young king, Alfonso, in 1495, to Messina; then went to Corfu; and in 1496 settled in Monopoli, and lastly in 1503 settled in Venice, where his servic
2 CONC es were employed in negotiating a commercial treaty between Portugal and the Venetian republic.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several times during the mid-to-late 15th century, he personally spent large amounts of his personal fortunes to bribe the Spanish Monarchy to permit the Jews to remain in Spain. It is claimed that Abarbanel offered them 600,000 c
2 CONC rowns for the revocation of the edict. It is said also that Ferdinand hesitated, but was prevented from accepting the offer by Torquemada, the grand inquisitor, who dashed into the royal presence and, throwing a crucifix down befo
2 CONC re the king and queen, asked whether, like Judas, they would betray their Lord for money. In the end, he managed only to get the date for the expulsion to be extended by two days.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Venice in 1508 and was buried in Padua next to Rabbi Judah Minz, Rabbi of Padua. Owing to the destruction of the Jewish cemetery there during the Siege of Padua in 1509, his grave is now unknown.
