1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Gans '''David ben Solomon (Shlomo) ben Seligman Gans'''] (German: David ben Salomon ben Seligmann Gans; also Ganz; Hebrew: דָּוִד בֶּן שְׁלֹמֹה גנז‎; 1541, Lippstadt - 25 August 
2 CONC 1613, Prague) was a Jewish mathematician, historian, astronomer, astrologer, and is best known for the works Tzemach David (1592) and Nechmad ve'naim.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Lippstadt, in what is now North Rhine-Westphalia, Germany, where his family were moneylenders. He studied rabbinical literature in Bonn and Frankfurt am Main, then in Kraków where he studied under Moses Isserles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later he attended the lectures of the Maharal of Prague and of his brother, R. Sinai. They introduced philosophy, mathematics, and astronomy into the circle of their studies, and from them Gans received the impulse to devote himse
2 CONC lf to these branches of science. He lived for a time at Nordheim (where he studied Euclid), passed several years in his native city of Lippstadt, and then in about 1564 settled at Prague. There he came into contact with Kepler and
2 CONC  Tycho Brahe, and took part for three consecutive days in astronomical observations at the Prague observatory. He also carried on a scientific correspondence with Johann Müller (Regiomontanus), and was charged by Tycho Brahe with 
2 CONC the translation of the Alfonsine Tables from Hebrew into German.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''His History'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Among Gans's works the most widely known is his history entitled Tzemach David, published first at Prague, in 1592. It is divided into two parts, the first containing the annals of Jewish history, the second those of general histo
2 CONC ry. The author consulted for the second part of his work the writings of Spangenberg, Laurentius Faustus, Hubertus Holtzius, Georg Cassino, and Martin Borisk. Though Gans's annals are very dry and have no great intrinsic value, th
2 CONC ey are memorable as the first work of this kind among the German Jews, who at that time appreciated historical knowledge but slightly. Indeed, in his preface to the second volume the author deemed it necessary to justify himself f
2 CONC or having dealt with so profane a subject as the annals ofgeneral history, and endeavored to demonstrate that it was permitted to read history on Saturdays. The Tzemach David passed through many editions. To the edition of Frankfo
2 CONC rt-on-the-Main, 1692, David ben Moses Rheindorf added a third part containing the annals of that century, which addition has been retained in later editions of the Tzemach. The first part of Gans's work, and extracts from the seco
2 CONC nd, were translated into Latin by Wilhelm Heinrich Vorst (Leyden, 1644). It was translated also into Judæo-German by Solomon Hanau (Frankfort-on-the-Main, 1692).
2 CONT
2 CONT Gans was also the author of: Gebulat ha-Eretz, a work on cosmography, which is in all probability identical with the Zurat ha-Eretz, published at Constantinople under the name of "David Abzi" ("Auza" = = "Gans"); Magen David, an a
2 CONC stronomical treatise, a part of which is included in the Nechmad ve'naim mentioned below; the mathematical works Ma'or ha-Ḳatan, Migdal David, and Prozdor, which are no longer in existence; Nechmad ve'naim. dealing with astronomy 
2 CONC and mathematical geography, published with additions by Joel ben Jekuthiel of Glogau at Jessnitz, 1743. This work is divided into 12 chapters and 305 paragraphs. In the introduction the author gives a historical survey of the deve
2 CONC lopment of astronomy and mathematical geography among the nations. Although acquainted with the work of Copernicus, Gans followed the Ptolemaic system, attributing the Copernican system to the Pythagoreans. He also ventures to ass
2 CONC ert that the prophet Daniel made a mistake in computation. A Latin translation of the introduction, and a résumé made by Hebenstreit, are appended to the Nechmad ve'naim.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death'''
2 CONT
2 CONT His grave is in Prague, is marked with a "Magen David" and a goose (German: Gans meaning goose in German).
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://he.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D7%93%D7%95%D7%93_%D7%92%D7%A0%D7%96 '''דוד גאנז''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Author of Tzemach Dovid: http://www.hebrewbooks.org/21930<br/>
2 CONT Author of Nechmad Ve'naim: http://www.hebrewbooks.org/14299
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- David ben Solomon (Shlomo) ben Seligman Gans (German: David ben Salomon ben Seligmann Gans; also Ganz; Hebrew: דָּוִד בֶּן שְׁלֹמֹה גנז‎; 1541, Lippstadt - August 25, 1613, Prague) was a Jewish mathematician, 
2 CONC historian, astronomer, astrologer, and is best known for the works Tzemach David (1592) and Nechmad ve'naim.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Lippstadt, in what is now North Rhine-Westphalia, Germany, where his family were moneylenders. He studied rabbinical literature in Bonn and then in Kraków where he studied under Moses Isserles. In 1564, he moved to 
2 CONC Prague. In Prague, he came into contact with Kepler and Tycho Brahe, and became a student of the Maharal.
2 CONT
2 CONT His grave is in Prague, is marked with a "Magen David" and a goose (German: Gans meaning goose in German).<br/>
2 CONT <br/>http://www.archive.org/stream/berhmteisrael02kohu#page/200/mode/2up<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT  Ludwig Herz, Die sechshundertjährige Geschichte der Familie Gans, Berlin 1934
2 CONT
2 CONT https://cs.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Gans
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Gans
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/2591/david-gans
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography (more complex, both history and astronomy):
2 CONT
2 CONT Andre Neher: David Gans, 1541-1613 : disciple du Maharal, assistant de Tycho Brahe et de Jean Kepler, 1974. English translation as Jewish thought and the scientific revolution of the sixteenth century : David Gans (1541-1613) and 
2 CONC his times (1986)
2 CONT
2 CONT Monography (focused to Gans as historian):
2 CONT
2 CONT Jiřina Šedinová: David Gans - Pražský renesanční židovský historik (David Gans - Prague Renaissance Jewish historian); Prague 2016, in Czech
