1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_HaLevi_Segal '''David haLevi Segal'''], the famous author of Turei Zahav (TaZ/TuZ)
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://he.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D7%93%D7%95%D7%93_%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9C '''דוד הלוי סגל''']
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT David HaLevi Segal,  '''דוד הלוי סגל''' ,wrote a commentary on Joseph Caro's Shulchan Aruch (Yoreh De'ah), which he published in Lublin in 1646. This commentary, known as the Turei Zahav ("Rows of Gold"), was accepted as one of th
2 CONC e highest authorities on Jewish law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thereafter, Segal became known by the acronym of his work, the TaZ, also known as the Taz/Tuz - ט"ז) בעל הטו"ז) or the Turei Zahav.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Ludmir, Volhynia (c. 1586–1667), Segal was the son of Samuel HaLevi Segal. His chief Torah teacher was his older brother, Isaac. He became a reputed Talmudic scholar, and married the daughter of Rabbi Joel Sirkes of Brest,
2 CONC  The Bach, whom he frequently quoted in his works.
2 CONT
2 CONT After residing with his father-in-law and continuing his Torah studies for several years, Segal and his family moved to Kraków. He was then appointed chief rabbi of Potelych (Polish Potylicz), near Rava, where he lived in great po
2 CONC verty. Later he went to Poznań, where he remained for several years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1641 he became Rav of the old community of Ostrog, or Ostroh, in Volhynia. There Segal established a famous yeshiva, and was soon recognized as one of the great halakhic authorities of his time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Ostrog, Segal wrote a commentary on Joseph Caro's Shulchan Aruch (Yoreh De'ah), which he published in Lublin in 1646. This commentary, known as the Turei Zahav ("Rows of Gold"), was accepted as one of the highest authorities on
2 CONC  Jewish law. Thereafter, Segal became known by the acronym of his work, the TaZ.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years after the publication of his commentary, Segal and his family had to flee the massacres of the Cossack insurrection under Bogdan Chmielnicki in 1648–1649. Segal went to Steinitz near Ostrau, Moravia, where he remained fo
2 CONC r some time. Not happy in Moravia, he returned to Poland as soon as order was restored, settling in Lemberg, where he remained for the rest of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Lemberg, Segal was appointed Av Beit Din (head of the rabbinical court). When Rabbi Meïr Sack, chief rabbi of Lemberg, died in 1653, he succeeded him in this position as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT Segal's last days were saddened by the death of his two sons, Mordechai and Solomon, who were killed in the riots occurring in Lemberg in the spring of 1664. His wife had died long before; now Segal married the widow of her brothe
2 CONC r, Samuel Hirz, Rav of Pińczów.
2 CONT
2 CONT His third son from his first marriage, Isaiah, and his stepson, Aryeh Löb, were the two Polish scholars who were sent — probably by Segal, or at least with his consent — to Turkey in 1666 to investigate the claims of the pseudo-Me
2 CONC ssiah, Shabbetai Tzvi. The two returned with a present for Segal from Shabbetai Tzvi — a white silk robe, along with a letter in which the latter promised to avenge the wrongs of the Jews of Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Segal's descendants were the Russian rabbinical family Paltrowitch, which produced 33 rabbis over several generations; American actress Gwyneth Paltrow descends from said family.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Works'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of Segal's works were published long after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Turei Zahav (טורי זהב - "Rows of Gold"), an indispensable commentary on Shulchan Aruch (Orach Chayim), was published by Shabbethai Bass in Dyhernfurth in 1692, together with the Magen Abraham by Abraham Abele Gumbiner.
2 CONT
2 CONT The title Turei Zahav is a play on the similar-sounding turei zahav (תורי זהב), "towers of gold", in Song of Songs 1:11. The title is abbreviated as Taz (ט"ז), and subtitled Magen David ("Shield of David", after Segal's first name
2 CONC ) in many editions. Both commentaries (Taz and Magen Abraham), together with the main text, the Shulchan Aruch, were republished frequently with several other commentaries, and still hold first rank among halakhic authorities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years before the publication of this work, Judel of Kovli, in Volhynia, a kabbalist and Talmudic scholar who wrote a commentary on Orach Chaim, gave money to have it published together with the Taz. His wishes were never carri
2 CONC ed out, and his money was used to publish another of Segal's works, Divrei David ("The Words of David"), a supercommentary on Rashi (Dyhernfurth, 1690).
2 CONT
2 CONT Part of the Taz on Shulchan Aruch (Chosen Mishpat, to ch. ccxcvi), appeared separately in Hamburg in the same year, with notes by Tzvi Ashkenazi. The other half, in spite of various attempts and general demand, did not appear unti
2 CONC l about seventy years later (Berlin, 1761). The Taz on Shulchan Aruch (Eben ha-Ezer), which was utilized in manuscript by Samuel ben Phoebus, the author of Bet Shemuel on the same part of the Shulchan Aruch, was first printed in Z
2 CONC olkiev in 1754.
2 CONT
2 CONT Segal also authored responsa which, though sometimes quoted from the manuscripts, were never published. He and Shabbethai Kohen (the ShaK) are the greatest halakhic authorities among the Acharonim; their decisions are of greater i
2 CONC mportance than those of Joseph Caro or of the Rema. In 1683, the Council of Four Lands declared that the authority of the Taz should be considered greater than that of the ShaK, but later the ShaK gained more and more in authority
2 CONC .
2 CONT
