1 NOTE {geni:about_me} An old, widely ramified family counting many rabbis among its members, who were and are still found in Italy, Poland, Germany, Alsace, and also in America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It derived its name from the locality of Katzenelnbogen in the Prussian province of Hesse-Nassau. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1312 Count Diether of Katzenelnbogen received permission from Emperor Henry VII. to keep twelve Jews at Katzenelnbogen; and Ludwig the Bavarian in 1330 permitted Count Wilhelm and his heirs to keep twenty-four Jews in their dom
2 CONC inions (Böhmer, "Regesten," No. 501; Wiener, "Regesten," pp. 23, 33).
