3 CITY Mayence
3 STAE Rhineland-Palatinate
3 CTRY Germany
1 OCCU Also called "Charif"
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/3631-brandeis-moses
2 CONT
2 CONT BRANDEIS, MOSES (surnamed Ḥarif = "The Keen Talmudist"):
2 CONT
2 CONT German rabbi and Talmudic teacher; born about 1685; died June 24, 1761 (This is a typo. He died June 24th 1767), in Mayence. As his surname indicates, he was famous in his time as a Talmudic dialectician, but he left no writings. 
2 CONC His father, Jacob Brandeis, was rabbi in Zorek, Russia Minor (perhaps, Zaryechye in the province of Poltava; see Walden, "Shem ha-Gedolim he-Ḥadash," i. 61). Moses studied at the yeshibah of Abraham Broda in Prague, and then direc
2 CONC ted a yeshibah in Fürth, Bavaria, which was supportedby his father-in-law, Gabriel ha-Levi, a rich philanthropist of that place. At the same time he performed the functions of a rabbi at Schnittach, a small place near Fürth. In 17
2 CONC 17 he went to Jung-Bunzlau, Bohemia, as chief rabbi, and in 1733 became rabbi of Mayence. Moses was also a cabalist, observing strictly the rules of life laid down by Isaac Luria.
2 CONT
2 CONT His sons were: Bezaleel Brandeis; Gabriel Brandeis, rabbi in Prague; and Jacob Brandeis, rabbi in Düsseldorf.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography:
2 CONT * Kaufmann, in Monatsschrift, xxxvii. 385 et seq.;
2 CONT * Baruch Judah Brandeis, Leshon Ḥakamim, Preface.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Die Inschriften des alten Judenfriedhofes in Wien- by Dr. Bernhard Wachstein - Band II- pg. 90, no.11
