1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Note: Abraham Kahana (d 1741) should not be confused with Abraham Areyh Lieb ben Shalom Shakhna Kahana (Loeb Scheines) who died in 1788. Abraham Areyh Lieb Kahana (Loeb Scheines)(d.1788) is the grand nephew of Abra
2 CONC ham Kahana (d 1741). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  In Toldot Yehudi Brody, Abraham Kahana's father is identified as Shalom Shachna Kahana.   In the Jewish Encylcopedia (1906) Abraham Kahana's father is identified as Samuel Kahana.  In Sefer Mazkeret li-gedole Ostraha, Menachem
2 CONC  Mendel Biber page 84 the author states that there are in fact two alternative genealogies for Abraham Kahana and that it is not clear if Abraham Kahana's father is Shalom Shachna Kahana or Samuel Kahana.    
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  Various dates are given for Abraham Kahana's death.   In Sefer Mazkeret li-gedole Ostraha  the date of death is 1735.  In the Jewish Encyclopedia the date of death is 1741.   In the Unbroken Chain the date of death is 1749.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.   Jewish Encyclopedia "Abraham b. Samuel Kahana, died 1741; Rabbi Dubno, previously rabbi of Brody and Ostrog (Volhynia). Rabbi Abraham Cahana b. Rabbi Shalom Shachna, a parnas of the Cracow community. He presided in Brody unti
2 CONC l 1718 when he was called to preside over Ostrog (Ostraha). However, he was forced to leave when an overlord of the city coveted his daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  Meorei Galicia (Encyclopedia of Galacian Sages) By Rabbi Meir Wunder: Volume 3, Page 321.
2 CONT
2 CONT The information regarding Abraham  Kahana is found under the heading for Abraham Aryeh Lieb Kahana (1725 - 1788).
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  Note:  Abraham Kahana (d. 1741) is also referred to on Geni as Avraham Kahana AB"D Duvne and was entered by Yakov Leib Grunfeld. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.   Abraham Kahana took part in the deliberations of the Council of Four Lands and signed decrees "in the years 1718, twice in 1724 and in 1739."  Source:  "Ostrog"  Encylopedia of Jewish Communities in Poland, Volume V (Ostroh, 
2 CONC Ukraine).   Translation of "Ostrog Chapter from Pinkas Hakehillot Polin.  http://www.jewishgen.org/yizkor/pinkas_poland/pol5_00034.html.   
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.    From the Jewish Encyclopedia (1906)  
2 CONT
2 CONT The following list of Dubno rabbis extends from 1600 to the present time: Isaiah ha-Levi Hurwitz (1600-06), author of "Shene Luḥot ha-Berit." Samuel b. Aaron ha-Levi Hurwitz (1625-30), cousin of Isaiah Hurwitz. Ẓebi (Hirsch) b. Oz
2 CONC er, son-in-law of Abraham Ḥayyim Shor, chief rabbi of Satanow; author of . Meïr b. Moses Ashkenazi, the father of Shabbethai Kohen (ShaK); died at Dubno Nov. 25, 1649. Judah ha-Ḥasid, martyred 1649. Abraham Heilprin (1660-62), son
2 CONC -in-law of the physician Jehiel Michael Epstein. Naḥman b. Meïr ha-Kohen Rapoport (also called Naḥman Lifsches); died in 1674; previously rabbi of Kremenetz (Volhynia) and Belz (Galicia); took part in the Council of Four Lands at 
2 CONC the fair of Jaroslaw. Moses b. Joseph, died at Lemberg May 22, 1684. Israel b. Mordecai Yolis (also called Israel Swinhar). Simḥah b. Naḥman ha-Kohen Rapoport, died at Szebreczin July 15, 1717; son-in-law of Israel b. Mordecai; re
2 CONC placed the latter in the rabbinate of Dubno from 1682 to 1688; rabbi of Grodno to 1714, of Lublin to 1717; called to the rabbinate of Lemberg in the same year; he died on his way there. Joseph b. Judah Yüdel of Lublin, died April 
2 CONC 13, 1706; wrote a work entitled "Ne'imah Ḳedoshah," containing moral precepts and a poem for the Sabbath. Samuel b. Shalom Shakna of Cracow, died at Brody June 22, 1729. Isaac b. Saul Ginzburg (1712-15). Eleazar b. Issachar Baer o
2 CONC f Cracow (1715-1719), maternal grandfather of Ezekiel Landau. Heschel b. Eleazar (also called R. Heschel "der Kleiner"), died July 25, 1729. Zalman Ephraim b. Saul. '''Abraham b. Samuel Kahana, died 1741; previously rabbi of Brody
2 CONC  and Ostrog (Volhynia).''' IsaacMoses b. Abraham Kahana (d. 1745). Saul b. Aryeh Löb, born at Reischo 1717; died at Amsterdam June 19, 1790; son-in-law of Abraham Kahana and author of "Binyan Ariel" (1745-55). Naphtali Herz b. Ẓeb
2 CONC i Hirsch (d. May 17, 1777). Ze'eb Wolf b. Naphtali Herz, born at Brody 1745; died at Dubno 1800; previously rabbi of Radzivil, Volhynia; his responsa were published in the "Tif'eret Ẓebi" of Ẓebi Hirsch, rabbi of Brody (Lemberg, 1
2 CONC 811). Nathan ha-Levi Hurwitz. Ḥayyim Mordecai Margaliot, brother-in-law of Nathan Hurwitz and author of "Sha'are Teshubah." Ḥayyim Jacob b. Ze'eb Wolf, previously rabbi of Rovno, Volhynia; died Sept. 25, 1849. David Ẓebi Auerbach,
2 CONC  son-in-law of Ḥayyim Jacob and author of "Malbushe Ṭaharah" (unpublished). Menahem Mendel Auerbach, son of David Ẓebi, is the present (1903) incumbent
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia: History of the Jews in Brody:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The first known rabbis of Brody were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Saul Katznellengogen Isaak Krakower Abrahame Kahane..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "
