0 @N6000000004339598093@ NOTE From Jewish Encyclopedia:
1 CONT
1 CONT ISSERLES, MOSES BEN ISRAEL (ReMA):
1 CONT
1 CONT Polish rabbi, code annotator, and philosopher; born at Cracow about 1520; died there May 1, 1572. His father was a rich and prominent Talmudist, and it may be concluded from the term "ha-parnes" which his son applies to him, that 
1 CONC he was the chief of the community. Isserles studied in his native city, and then under Shalom Shekna, rabbi of Lublin, whose son-in-law he became. Among his fellow pupils were his relative Solomon Luria (MaHaRShaL), and Chayyim b.
1 CONC  Bezaleel, who later was his opponent. Isserles returned to Cracow about 1550, when he established a large yeshibah and, being a wealthy man, supported his pupils at his own cost. Three years later he was ordained rabbi and was na
1 CONC med one of the three dayyanim to form the rabbinate of Cracow, which community had as yet no chief rabbi ("ab bet din"). In 1556, when the plague ravaged Cracow, Isserles went to Szydlowiec, where he wrote his "Me?ir Yayin."
1 CONT
1 CONT While still young Isserles was recognized as an authority in rabbinical matters. As early as 1550 his relative Meïr Katzenellenbogen of Padua, a man of eighty years, had applied to him to use his influence in forbidding the unlawf
1 CONC ul printing in Poland of the "Mishneh Torah," which was causing Katzenellenbogen heavy loss. Isserles in ten responsa defended the interests of the aged rabbi of Padua. He also corresponded with many other rabbis, among them Josep
1 CONC h Caro, who answered him in a very considerate manner.
1 CONT
1 CONT Relations with Solomon Luria.
1 CONT
1 CONT A close friendship existed between Isserles and his relative Luria, though, as will be seen, they differed later on various matters. In many respects their aims were similar: both aimed at the truth in their decisions, both worked
1 CONC  for the furtherance of Talmudic literature, and both ascribed great importance to customs ("minhagim"). In certain other matters, however, there was great opposition between the two friends, especially in their attitude toward ph
1 CONC ilosophy. Luria was the adversary of philosophy; Isserles, its warm defender, declaring openly that the aim of man is to search for the cause and the meaning of things. Isserles accordingly devoted a part of his time to philosophy
1 CONC . When Luria reproached him for having based his decisions on Aristotle's teachings, he replied that he followed Maimonides, and that he studied Greek philosophy only from the "Moreh"; further, that he pursued his philosophical st
1 CONC udies on Sabbaths and holy days only, when people generally took walks, and that it was better to occupy oneself with philosophy than to err through Cabala. The fact that Isserles studied the "Moreh" on Sabbaths and holy days-on w
1 CONC hich days the reading of profane literature was particularly forbidden-shows how much he appreciated philosophy in general and Maimonides in particular.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isserles also occupied himself with the secular sciences; and whereas Caro says that a man must devote all his time to the study of the Torah and the Talmud, Isserles decides that one may now and then occupy himself with the secul
1 CONC ar sciences provided he is not led into heresy through studying them. He himself had an extensive knowledge of astronomy and a great liking for history.  Isserles was opposed to "pilpul" ; and he taught his pupils how to interpret
1 CONC  the Talmud in a simple way.
1 CONT
1 CONT Connection with Council of Four Lands.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some of his responsa are written in an elevated style of versified prose, as are his prefaces, though, as he himself declared, he had never studied Hebrew grammar.  Isserles was also an excellent scribe, and in the scroll of the P
1 CONC entateuch which he wrote and which is preserved in the synagogue - originally his house, which he gave to the community for a place of worship-there are fourteen readings different from those in other scrolls. Isserles was promine
1 CONC nt in the Council of Four Lands, which was established in his time. A quarrel having broken out among the rabbis, he launched an anathema against those who were desirous of continuing the agitation and would not await the decision
1 CONC  of the rabbinical congress at the fair of Lublin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Owing to the fact that he was one of the founders of rabbinic learning in Poland and Germany, and was recognized as the authority not only in rabbinic law but also in Cabala, philosophy, and the secular sciences, legends attached 
1 CONC themselves to him. Many curious stories are told of the number of years he lived, of the number of works he wrote, etc. Even at the present day the Polish Jews consider him a saint, and on the anniversary of his death large number
1 CONC s make a pilgrimage to his tomb at Cracow. In the epitaph on Isserles' tombstone occurs the following: "From Moses [Maimonides] to Moses [Isserles] there was none like Moses". This is an exaggeration, but there is a resemblance be
1 CONC tween the two, as both were halakists, and both devoted a large part of their time to philosophy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isserles and Caro.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isserles' writings may be divided into two classes of works: (1) halakic, and (2) philosophical, cabalistic, exegetical, and scientific. It is on the former that his great reputation rests. His zeal for the Law and his vindication
1 CONC  of Ashkenazic customs spread his fame far and wide. Indeed, he may with justice be called the Ashkenazic codifier; for he was to the Ashkenazim what Caro was to the Sephardim. Like Caro, he wrote a commentary to the Arba' Turim, 
1 CONC entitled "Darke Mosheh," of which two parts were printed. This commentary contains a severe criticism of the "Bet Yosef." It is also the source of Isserles' other work, "Mappah," which is both a criticism of and a supplement to Ca
1 CONC ro's Shulchan 'Aruk. Isserles saw that Caro's "table" was not sufficiently "prepared"; for Caro as a Sephardi had neglected the Ashkenazic minhagim. He therefore provided the Shulchan 'Aruk (= "Prepared Table") with a "Mappah" (= 
1 CONC "Table-Cloth"), consisting of notes ("haggahot") inserted in Caro's text. These notes first appeared in the Cracow edition of the Shulchan 'Aruk (1571), in Rashi type to distinguish them from the text of Caro.
1 CONT
1 CONT The authorities receiving special attention in the "Darke Mosheh" and "Mappah" are the Acharonim and, more particularly, the minhagim, to which Isserles attached great importance. The importance of the minhag had already been poin
1 CONC ted out by Solomon Luria, who declared that the minhag outweighed the Law. Isserles went still further: he established the minhag in several cases as the standard authority. "The minhag is the Law," he said. "One must not act cont
1 CONC rary to the minhag". Still, even in establishing the minhag as an authority, he did not do so indiscriminately, because he made a distinction among minhagim. Where the minhag seemed to him absurd, he declared it to be unacceptable
1 CONC . It must be added that when Ashkenazim now speak of the Shulchan 'Aruk they understand by it both Caro's text and Isserles' notes, and that when there is a conflict between the two, Isserles is taken as the authority.
1 CONT
1 CONT Liberal Tendencies.
1 CONT
1 CONT As to Isserles' system, it may be said that he was more inclined toward restrictive decisions ("machmir"), especially in his rulings concerning kasher food. But he has been judged too severely by modern Maskilim, who have accused 
1 CONC him of making arbitrary restrictions, of inventing customs, and of causing heavy pecuniary losses by his reliance on the minhagim. These accusations are unjustified, because Isserles was consistent in principle, in as much as he r
1 CONC egarded the minhag as the norm for the practise on both the liberal and the restrictive sides. Throughout his "Darke Mosheh," "Mappah," and responsa occur many liberal decisions of his which are based on the minhag, but are contra
1 CONC ry to the decisions of other casuists ("posekim"), including Solomon Luria. In general he adapted his decisions to the spirit of the time in which he lived; and he gave a liberal decision when he saw that a restrictive one would p
1 CONC rove burdensome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isserles touches also, in his halakic decisions, on the question of the superiority of the Hebrew language and the sacred characters. He allows one to read on Saturdays non-religious works if written in Hebrew . The Targumim have 
1 CONC the same sacred character as the Hebrew. The square characters are sacred because the scroll of the Law is written in them; and he forbade the writing of non-religious works in such characters.
0 @N6000000009317582019@ NOTE http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/view.jsp?artid=366&letter=I&search=Moses%20Isserles
0 @N6000000003327798837@ NOTE
1 CONT G5.2. Moses, A.B.D. of Lubjeme and Chelm, was also from the first  marriage. He married Sarah, daughter of R. Benjamin Benas of Posen, known as Benas  Reb Mendels. R. Moses' approbations appeared in Damesek Eliezer (Lublin, 1646) 
1 CONC by R. Eliezer, son of Samuel, A.B.D. Apt (Opatow), and Ahavat Tzion (Lublin, 1639) by R. Abraham, son of Moses Ashkenazi Heilprin, who married his  first cousin, the daughter of Judah Katzenellenbogen. He died some time after 1643
1 CONC .
